From: ROCKNIX local <local@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] input: rsinput: parse oversized RX chunks instead of dropping them

On s2idle resume the MCU powers up streaming, the tty flush work lags, and
receive_buf gets >1024 bytes at once. The driver dropped the whole chunk,
including the version reply, so the resume handshake timed out and the pad
stayed in its default stream mode. Parse large chunks in slices instead.
Also ignore RX until the input device exists (rebind NULL deref).
---
--- a/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c	2026-10-08 10:26:34.503202344 +0300
+++ b/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c	2026-10-08 10:26:34.510326115 +0300
@@ -504,8 +504,30 @@
         return count;
     }
 
-    if (count > sizeof(drv->rx_buf)) {
-        dev_warn_ratelimited(&serdev->dev, "RX buffer overflow\n");
+    /*
+     * The client ops outlive an unbind, so on a rebind bytes can arrive
+     * after probe set drvdata but before the input device exists. Drop
+     * them; the MCU is re-initialised by probe anyway.
+     */
+    if (!READ_ONCE(drv->input))
+        return count;
+
+    /*
+     * After power-up the MCU free-runs at a high rate, and during resume the
+     * tty flush work can fall behind and hand us more than rx_buf holds in a
+     * single call. Dropping the whole chunk also dropped the version reply
+     * buried in it, so the resume handshake timed out. Parse it in slices
+     * instead; each slice fits rx_buf alongside a partial frame.
+     */
+    if (count > sizeof(drv->rx_buf) / 2) {
+        size_t off = 0;
+
+        while (off < count) {
+            size_t n = min(count - off, sizeof(drv->rx_buf) / 2);
+
+            rsinput_rx(serdev, data + off, n);
+            off += n;
+        }
         return count;
     }
 
